-- title:
--   At most (p-1)μ/12 supersingular places on X₁(M)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, and let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$. Write $F =$ [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) $\Omega\,M$ for the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\Omega((q))$ over $\Omega$ generated by the ratios of integral forms `intFormRatiosC` for $\Gamma_1(M)$, and let $\bar\jmath \in F$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series is [`ModularCurve.jqModC`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) $\Omega$, i.e. $q^{-1}$ times the reduction to $\Omega$ of the power series $E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$. A place of $F$ over $\Omega$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $\Omega$, distinct from $F$ itself and a principal ideal ring; for such a place $v$ and $f$ in its valuation ring, $v.\mathrm{evalAt}\,f$ is the element of $\Omega$ mapping to the residue of $f$ in the residue field, under the inverse of the structure map. The assertion is that twelve times the number of places $v$ of $F$ over $\Omega$ such that $\bar\jmath$ lies in the valuation ring of $v$ and $v.\mathrm{evalAt}\,\bar\jmath$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7) $p\,\Omega$ — the set of $j \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $j$ has no nonzero point $P$ with $p \cdot P = 0$ — is at most $(p-1)$ times the index of $\Gamma_1(M) \sqcup \langle -1 \rangle$ in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ (the subgroup generated by $\Gamma_1(M)$ and $-I$). The count is taken as a `Nat.card`, so it is $0$ if the set of such places is infinite.
--
--   This is the upper bound in the count of supersingular points on the fine modular curve $X_1(M)$ in characteristic $p$: the number of places where $\bar\jmath$ is regular with supersingular value is at most $(p-1)\mu/12$, where $\mu$ is the index of $\pm\Gamma_1(M)$ in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$. It is used, together with the matching lower bound, in the genus comparison between $X_1(M)$ in characteristic $p$ and the Igusa curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_twelve_mul_natCard_evalAt_mem_ssJSet_le_sub_one_mul_index_gamma1_x1FunctionFieldC.lean

